% ============================================================================
% earth-layers — Earth's four internal layers → the physical STATE each one is
% in (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of earth science, and a rung a GEOLOGY (or plate-
% tectonics, or earthquake) agent reasons UP from: our planet is built out of
% concentric layers, and each layer is in a characteristic PHYSICAL STATE.
% Cut the Earth open like a boiled egg and, from the outside in, you meet: a
% thin RIGID crust (the shell), a hot SEMI-SOLID mantle (the white), a LIQUID
% outer core, and a SOLID inner core (the two-part yolk). Before you can reason
% about how seismic waves travel, why the outer core alone can generate a
% magnetic field, or how the mantle "flows," you must first know WHICH state a
% given layer is in. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? has_state(outer_core, $State)      % liquid
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `has_state(layer, state)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the state
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a word
% that is not one of these four internal layers (it never invents a state).
%
% Each of the four internal layers and the physical state the source states for
% it, as a little truth table:
%
%     layer         state (source's own word)   a beginner's cue
%     -----------   -------------------------   -------------------------------
%     crust         rigid                       thin brittle outer shell of rock
%     mantle        semi_solid                  hot dense rock that slowly flows
%     outer_core    liquid                      molten iron-nickel; spins → B field
%     inner_core    solid                       iron-nickel kept solid by pressure
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a state and recall which layer is in it:
%
%     ? has_state($Layer, liquid)   % outer_core
%
% A word that is NOT one of these four internal layers — `atmosphere`,
% `lithosphere`, `ocean` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a layer recall ABSTAINS
% on it rather than inventing a state. (The lithosphere and asthenosphere are
% real subdivisions the source names, but they are not one of the four primary
% layers this table indexes, so they abstain too.) That honest-abstention
% property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers
% exactly the layer→state pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `state` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be the
% state word the source EXPLICITLY uses for that layer — never one the source
% does not support. Where the everyday word differs from the source's word, the
% atom uses the SOURCE'S OWN word, so every value stays literally checkable
% against the quoted span: the crust is called "rigid" (not "solid"), and the
% mantle is called "semi-solid" (not "molten"). That literal fidelity is why
% the crust cell is `rigid` and the mantle cell is `semi_solid`.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every layer→state pair
% below was read out of a fetched USGS "This Dynamic Earth" page, and any pair
% whose state word could not be verified there would have been dropped). All
% four spans come from the SAME primary U.S. Geological Survey publication:
%
%   crust → rigid
%     https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/inside.html
%     (USGS — "This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics", U.S. Geological Survey)
%     "The crust, the outermost layer, is rigid and very thin compared with the
%      other two." (also: "Like the shell of an egg, the Earth's crust is brittle
%      and can break.")
%
%   mantle → semi_solid
%     https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/inside.html
%     "Below the crust is the mantle, a dense, hot layer of semi-solid rock
%      approximately 2,900 km thick."
%
%   outer_core → liquid   and   inner_core → solid
%     https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/inside.html
%     "Unlike the yolk of an egg, however, the Earth's core is actually made up
%      of two distinct parts: a 2,200 km-thick liquid outer core and a 1,250
%      km-thick solid inner core."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the USGS statement that fixes BOTH
% core rows (outer_core → liquid, inner_core → solid) at once. Every OTHER row's
% per-fact source and verbatim span is listed above, so each state remains
% independently checkable. All spans are from the same USGS "This Dynamic Earth"
% publication, a primary U.S. government (U.S. Geological Survey) source, so
% `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table has_state {
    columns layer, state

    row (crust, rigid)          % "The crust, the outermost layer, is rigid and very thin"
    row (mantle, semi_solid)    % "a dense, hot layer of semi-solid rock"
    row (outer_core, liquid)    % "a 2,200 km-thick liquid outer core"
    row (inner_core, solid)     % "a 1,250 km-thick solid inner core"

    source "Unlike the yolk of an egg, however, the Earth's core is actually made up of two distinct parts: a 2,200 km-thick liquid outer core and a 1,250 km-thick solid inner core."
    locator "https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/inside.html"
    trust authoritative
}
